<p style="text-align: center;"><img src="/images/2012/Montcalm-Cloud-Study.jpg" alt="A painting study of clouds over fields" width="500" height="558" /></p>
<p>My efforts to paint cloud formations have met with very mixed results over the years. &nbsp;They often end up too heavy or, worse still, muddy.</p>
<p>With this study I set out to paint something more like real cloud formations.&nbsp;I painted the whole sky over with a thin layer of mid-tone grey mixed with white, black and a little ultramarine. &nbsp;I let it dry for a few weeks and then scumbled the lighter parts of the cloud formations with a mix of white, red and cad yellow diluted with lots of medium.</p>
<p>Overall this approach is MUCH easier than trying to paint the clouds alla prima and I like the way they turned out.</p>
<p>I based the study on a plein air painting that I had done at Montcalm Farm, Purcellville earlier this year.</p>